        <p>• The Liberal party managers are confident that a reaction strainer Toryien, has set in all over the country, and although Seliehury is the only vacant twat where a Liberal gain is expected, large reduc- tions in the majoritioa at Waltham.", and Rom- ford are confidently hoped for. As for Forfarshire, no doubt is entertained that the seat will he kept, pomeitly with no att•ance en the figures secured by the Into Liberal member. As a hopeful sign of I, turning confidence in the Liberal ranks, I hear that conferences, principally of members repro- minting provincial constituencies, bare been held lively at Parliamsnt.street, with the view of strengthening the Liberal poaition throughout the country. The idea is to eppoint a chief organiser in every county, whose duty it will be to stimulate activity in all the local Liheral ansociatione, and, without neeillevily interfering, to carry on est.-n- -aive propaganda work. A number of the richer men of the party have thrown thenteelves heart and soul into the prelect, and a beginning in this desirable direction will be made almost immediately in some of the northern counties.</p>
        <p>There is a gond deal of diosatiefartion expressed In come (parten about the distribution of the Drummond Castle fund. It is raid that enough lota been done kr Oh o Moline ilaber-folk without this latest diehursement for their new rainwater tank, and that the root of the money in hand rhould be dietributorl among the relationa of thoae who were drowned. Some nf thees relations were entirely dependent on the deed. One Poor woman wax dependent on her lion. She hn, lost her eon and her ineome, hn, nnt oven a solitary keep-rake saved from the week. Everything, for her, went down in the Drummond Caatle.</p>
