        <p>." Now, gentlomen, I want to tell you I for England for too Engliah. I want England nine first, second and third. and the foreigners mule a long way niter." The next day he lamed elr,tion address and in this he said I believe fingland for the English, and am opposed to immigration of pauper aliens to compete with , ;bah working men in our already crowded nu market." This, then, lath. latest flower of y patriUtiim. An Englishman of eight or nine .8 standing is to be ratimmsd to the House of nmons to preserve " our " labour market for Britiah workingman. Tbis election will be • y simple test of the reasoning capacity of the y workingmen of Rumford.</p>
