      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1897-01-29_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>AROUND THE M RDITER. RANEAN.</head>
        <p>AROUND THE M RDITER. RANEAN.</p>
        <p>GIRL RUN DOWN BY A SIIANDRA AT _NEWTON</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE Wei Al EettLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>(BS AN OCCASIONAL COBILNINNOINT),</p>
        <p>The Gilchrist lecture last week, which waa upon , Waves of Water and Waves of Light," woks patron- ised by the public to en extent far beyond the mom sanguine expectationlothe hall being uncomtortahly crowded in every part. Tea student of Ma branch of the sciences, the lecture was doubtless most i•- teresung. but to the ordinary public I am afraid it fell rather flat ; at any me it area act bear So ea- tenanting as the previous lecture hy Professor Levres.ol course the enters illestrations were very good, but the dinalifFill was WIL Oren In that from and easy style adopted by Professor Lewes, sod the attention of the audience did not appear to be en- chained.</p>
        <p>Whilst I am on this subject. I should just like to draw attention to the conditions whteli prevail in the Town Hall,during a concert or other gathering. The draughts in the Law Courts, London, are pro- verbial, but if they ire any worse than the draught In our Tow. Hall, then they must indeed be very bad. It is poaitively ouicidal to Pit anywhere in the halLin a direct line with the entrance doore, for I eerily believe it a person's head wu not so well ,aecured to the body as it is, it would be blown off when the said door. opened. Thin is not news,but a common source of complaint for thou who have • been so unfortunate an to experience it. I think eomething should be done to make the hall a little more cosy,and the Council could w.it do better than give the matter attention.</p>
        <p>Toe heavy downfsll of allow brought disappoint- ment to the hearts of many who were looking for- weed to • good spell of skating Prior to the snow the ponds in the district were in fairly good condi- tion, and many wren enjoying the exhilarating !exercise on the shallow places, but some foolhardy spirits ventured on Newton Ink., so early 44 Sunday. Fortunately for themselves the ice held good, and they were enabled to reach terra-firma with whole skins. It is all very well to run the risk of. wet- ting. with • possible loss of life, but the example is • had one, especially for youngsters, who are not supposed to be endowed with the same amount of common-sense as their elders, sad who might thoughtlessly place their liven in danger. Newton lake is well-known as • dangerous spot, and until it has been frozen over at least • week it should be left severely aloes.</p>
      </div>
